WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.480
The Mandalorian declared its intention to
hit its audience with twists and turns aplenty

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.230
with its first episode alone, and episode
2 titled "The Child" effectively doubled down

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.230
on that notion.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.780
Its final act had our jaws on the floor, and
we're champing at the bit to unpack all of

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.050
its implications.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.980
Before we do, however and just in case you
somehow missed the title of this video please

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.910
be advised that spoilers for episode 2 of
The Mandalorian follow.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.039
"Be careful."

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:27.179
"You too."

00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:32.250
The episode has our hero at last escaping
Arvala-7 with his would-be target, but everything

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.330
has changed; with the revelation that said
target is an infant and a member of the same

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:40.860
mysterious race of the Jedi Master Yoda, the
Mandalorian's life has grown infinitely more

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.070
complicated.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.730
After lulling the audience into a false sense
of security with fifteen solid minutes of

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:49.800
a seemingly-unrelated fetch quest for the
Mandalorian's ship parts, the episode answers

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.140
the question we were naturally led to ask
after the pilot in shocking fashion: is the

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.830
baby Force-sensitive, like Yoda?

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.830
If you listened closely during the climactic
scene, you could hear The Mandalorian's creator

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:04.559
Jon Favreau laughing his face off, because
of course the Yoda baby is Force-sensitive.

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:08.240
The show chose to reveal that fact in the
most sweeping, epic moment in an otherwise

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.380
very small and quiet episode.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.789
During an attempt to secure an egg from a
ferocious horned beast with which he hopes

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:18.211
to barter with the Jawas for his ship parts,
the baby uses the Force to save the Mandalorian

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:22.479
as he grimly stares down death at the hooves
of said beast with naught but a knife; this

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:26.409
completely shifts the narrative's paradigm,
and it's quite unclear if the Mandalorian

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:28.619
himself understands what has just happened.

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:32.689
There's a particularly sneaky bit of exposition
in the dialogue between the Mandalorian and

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:37.549
Kuiil just before the final ship repair montage:
when Kuiil asks if the baby was injured, the

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:39.399
Mandalorian replies no, at least not physically.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:44.039
Kuiil then asks the Mandalorian to explain
what happened again, that he doesn't understand,

00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:46.920
and the Mandalorian tellingly replies that
he doesn't know, either.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.929
The two never explicitly state it, but the
Mandalorian has apparently told Kuiil how

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:54.909
he retrieved the egg with the last-minute
miracle the baby provided by suddenly displaying

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:56.270
its Force abilities.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:00.680
We the audience, of course, know exactly what
happened and what it means, but if the Mandalorian

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.329
is telling Kuiil the truth, then the bounty
hunter doesn't know what the Force is, and

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:07.509
he has no idea what the baby is capable of
and why.

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:11.280
That's a pretty big deal, and it could have
a massive impact on the bounty hunter's actions

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.360
going forward.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.280
It's more reasonable than it seems at first
blush that the Mandalorian would be ignorant

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.280
of the Force.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.060
Consider: it's twenty-five years before the
ultimate fall of the Jedi Knights and the

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.170
rise of the First Order, we're following a
man living in the Outer Rim, and we know for

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.520
a fact that he is an orphan.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.730
Even fellow Outer Rim dweller Luke Skywalker
was completely ignorant of anything related

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.890
to the Force until Obi-Wan "Ben" Kenobi came
along, so there's no reason to think that

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.560
the Mandalorian would be any more knowledgeable
on the matter.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.579
The Mandalorian is also... well, Mandalorian,
so he probably has very little formal education

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.700
beyond his tactical and combat training.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.760
If you were saved by an indescribable feat
of supernatural power that can only be described

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.810
as miraculous, you'd probably be a little
shook, and you may even reassess your priorities.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.810
It's the classic bounty hunter conflict trope:
the Mandalorian has now learned a little too

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.780
much about his target, and it looks like he'll
be faced with a serious moral quandary as

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:02.780
a result.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:06.810
We also shouldn't forget that there is practically
an entire phalanx of bounty hunters looking

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.710
for the little Force-sensitive tyke.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:12.410
The Mandalorian has successfully completed
the most difficult part of the task removing

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.319
the target from its secured bunker and it
has suddenly become much, much easier for

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:18.790
these scoundrels to take the baby for themselves.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:22.820
The only thing now standing between the infant
and its many pursuers is the Mandalorian,

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.870
who should probably get used to having a giant
target on his back.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.010
None of his options at this point are particularly
attractive.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:33.180
Is the lure of Beskar steel too much for the
Mandalorian, versus the obvious moral issue

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.519
presented by handing the baby over to an emissary
of the Empire?

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:46.180
Is his life worth going rogue and protecting
the Yoda baby at all costs?

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.099
We can see that the Mandalorian operates according
to a code of honor and respect.

00:03:50.099 --> 00:03:54.319
That Beskar steel, though, represents not
cash, but a piece of his religion; a sacred

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:58.349
item that not only provides him with personal
enrichment, but the opportunity to help his

00:03:58.349 --> 00:04:00.040
people in a profound way.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:04.560
For that matter, it’s worth noting it remains
suspiciously convenient that the Client presented

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.670
the Mandalorian with exactly this form of
payment at a time that the bounty hunter was

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:11.230
shown to be particularly desperate for work.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:15.120
Heading into the third episode, the Mandalorian
is certainly between a rock and a hard place

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.130
and it's being painfully illustrated for him
that, as the Client stated when he took the

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.740
job, bounty hunting is indeed a complicated
profession.

